  "term": "principle of parsimony",
  "definition": "In this historical logical-psychological usage, the principle of parsimony is the idea that a sentence needs only one term to carry its reference to reality or objectivity. Stating that reference again in the predicate or other elements is treated as unnecessary repetition.",
